#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# poisson_bracket

#% text
# `poisson_bracket(f, g, q, p)` gives the Poisson bracket `sum (df/dq dg/dp - df/dp dg/dq)` of `f` and `g` over the conjugate pairs of the coordinates `q` and momenta `p`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `q` and `p` are names (or terms such as `x(t)`), single or as lists of equal length.

#% item
# `poisson_bracket(f, H, q, p)` is the rate of change of `f` along the flow of the Hamiltonian `H`: a function whose bracket with `H` vanishes is conserved.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
poisson_bracket(x, p, x, p)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# The angular momentum algebra `{L_x, L_y} = L_z`:

#% cell
poisson_bracket(y*pz - z*py, z*px - x*pz, [x, y, z], [px, py, pz])
#% output
#= py*x - px*y

#% cell
poisson_bracket(p^2/(2*m) + k*x^2/2, x, x, p)
#% output
#= -p/m

#% text
# A component of the Runge-Lenz vector is conserved in Kepler's problem:

#% cell
simplify(poisson_bracket(py*(x*py - y*px) - x/sqrt(x^2 + y^2), (px^2 + py^2)/2 - 1/sqrt(x^2 + y^2), [x, y], [px, py]))
#% output
#= 0

